I"�&<p>Imagine a creature that sees one of two cues and must choose left or right. It learns which choice pays off. Halfway through its life, the rule flips. Its old behavior now earns the wrong outcome, and it has to recover while continuing to act.</p>

<p>Cadence is a Python research library built for experiments like this. Each interaction gives the system a new observation and, when available, the outcome of its previous action. The network settles on its next action, and the same stream can also teach it. The repository includes an example where the reward rule reverses partway through a run. <a href="https://github.com/muellerberndt/cadence">Cadence repository</a> · <a href="https://github.com/muellerberndt/cadence/blob/main/docs/start.md#routine-and-repair-live">Continuous interaction example</a></p>

<p>In his article on embodied AGI, Cadence creator Bernhard Mueller puts the same problem in a kitchen. A robot has to learn what happens when it grips a particular cup or pulls on a drawer that sticks. Its experience has to carry forward into the next attempt, while the robot continues to operate within its compute budget. Cadence explores how recurrent activity, memory, and local updates could support that kind of learning. <a href="https://muellerberndt.medium.com/you-dont-need-attention-after-all-the-road-to-embodied-agi-with-cadence-8606a64e40df">Bernhard Mueller, The road to embodied AGI with Cadence</a></p>

