  <figcaption>One neuron adjusts its state in response to its inputs. The example illustrates a local update. <a href="https://muellerberndt.medium.com/you-dont-need-attention-after-all-the-road-to-embodied-agi-with-cadence-8606a64e40df#77ba">Diagram from Bernhard Mueller’s article</a>.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>A settled answer can still be wrong. The settlement check tells us how closely the current state satisfies the network’s equations. The reward tells us whether the choice actually worked. Cadence records the solver’s error and how many sweeps it needed, so a slow decision and a bad decision can be measured separately. <a href="https://github.com/muellerberndt/cadence/blob/main/docs/start.md#qualification-and-refusal">Settlement checks and refusal</a></p>

<h2 id="how-the-system-learns-from-a-correction">How the system learns from a correction</h2>

<p>Suppose a teacher shows the creature that a cue calls for the left action. Cadence can let the network settle on its own, then run additional settles with small pushes toward and away from the taught answer. Each connection’s update uses the difference in the activity of the neurons it joins across the nudged states. The rule comes from equilibrium propagation, a family of methods that uses differences between equilibria to estimate how weights should change. <a href="https://github.com/muellerberndt/cadence/blob/main/docs/settling.md#learning-the-freenudged-rule">Cadence’s learning rule</a> · <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.05179">Equilibrium Propagation</a></p>

<p>When the environment supplies a reward instead of the correct answer, it gives the system a different kind of information. A connection keeps a fading record of its recent contribution to action choices. When the outcome arrives, a shared signal compares the earlier prediction with the reward received plus the estimated value of what follows. That signal scales the updates to those connections. This gives a later outcome a way to affect a recent choice. <a href="https://github.com/muellerberndt/cadence/blob/main/docs/reward.md#eligibility-traces-and-dopamine">Cadence’s reward learning guide</a></p>

<p>In the continuing interaction mode, Cadence uses a rule it calls <em>arousal</em> to decide when to spend effort learning. In routine conditions it can choose an action after one settle. An unexpected outcome or a drop in reward can switch on exploration, learning, and memory writes. In the reversal example, the interesting part is what happens immediately after the change. An action that used to pay now fails, and the system can revise its behavior while the task is still running. <a href="https://github.com/muellerberndt/cadence/blob/main/docs/start.md#routine-and-repair-live">Routine and repair in Cadence</a></p>

<h2 id="three-kinds-of-change">Three kinds of change</h2>

<p>An action can change because the current observation is different, because the system remembers an earlier outcome, or because experience changed its connections. Cadence exposes those mechanisms separately. <a href="https://github.com/muellerberndt/cadence/blob/main/docs/settling.md#three-state-lifetimes">State lifetimes in Cadence</a></p>

