<p>That separation makes the reward reversal a better test than a final accuracy score alone. If the creature recovers, we can ask whether it recalled an earlier association or changed its policy through new feedback. A comparison with memory disabled or learning frozen could help tell those explanations apart.</p>

<h2 id="from-simulation-to-a-physical-robot">From simulation to a physical robot</h2>

<p>Mueller describes simulated bodies that learn from stored outcomes and a separate solver that reduces work when a change is local. Those results test parts of the design. The larger claim depends on putting them together in a physical robot that can learn a new task, adapt when conditions change, and retain older skills. The comparison would have to count the cost of settling, communication, memory, and learning as well as task success. <a href="https://muellerberndt.medium.com/you-dont-need-attention-after-all-the-road-to-embodied-agi-with-cadence-8606a64e40df#55f2">Mueller’s account of the evidence and open tests</a></p>

