<h3 id="what-the-equilibrium-comparison-tests">What the equilibrium comparison tests</h3>

<p>Cadence’s finite grammar benchmark compares its local update with an implicit equilibrium gradient on the same network. The task is to judge subject and verb agreement with a distractor noun between them, using supplied inputs that identify the words and their roles. The setup has 24 training sentences and eight test sentences. It tests whether the local update can learn a small nonlinear judgment from those inputs. The project separately describes full-sentence language as a research goal. <a href="https://github.com/muellerberndt/cadence/tree/main/benchmarks/local_equilibrium">Local equilibrium benchmark</a> · <a href="https://github.com/muellerberndt/cadence#why-cadence">Cadence research goals</a></p>

<p>The gradient connection depends on conditions such as smooth, stable equilibria and symmetric effective weights. At a finite nudge and with imperfect solves, the local contrast is an estimate. Evaluating efficiency also means counting the work of all the solves used to answer and learn, along with memory maintenance. For the reversal task, I would compare recovery time and the cost of each interaction against a recurrent learner given the same observations and rewards. <a href="https://github.com/muellerberndt/cadence#where-the-learning-signal-comes-from">Cadence’s learning assumptions and comparison</a></p>
